Lint Harness – Pre-Commit Quality Gate 

Technical Architecture and Design Documentation 

 

1. Purpose 

The Pre-Commit Lint Harness is a quality gate that runs before a commit is finalized. Its primary purpose is to prevent low-quality or policy-violating code from entering the shared repository while maintaining a fast developer feedback loop. 

The harness is designed around four key outcomes: 

Block commits that fail to define quality checks. 

Skip unnecessary validation when the changes cannot impact the guarded quality dimensions. 

Provide actionable failure context instead of exposing only raw command output. 

Support a guided fix-and-retry workflow with explicit human approval. 

2. Prerequisites 

The following prerequisites must be available before setting up or executing the Pre-Commit Lint Harness. 

Note: This workflow is currently designed to work with GitHub Copilot inside VS Code only. 

2.1 Core Prerequisites 

1. Git Repository 

The project must be hosted in a Git repository. The lint harness uses Git to identify files that may be affected by the current commit. 

git diff --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR HEAD — detects changed files, including staged and unstaged changes. 

git ls-files --others --exclude-standard — detects untracked files. 

2. Node.js & npm 

Node.js and npm must be installed and available in the system PATH. They are required to execute the project's lint commands. 

npm run affected:lint:ci — the main lint command used by the harness. 

3. Nx Monorepo Tools 

The project must use Nx where affected-project detection is required. Nx is used to identify impacted projects and execute the appropriate lint targets. 

npx nx show projects --affected --withTarget=lint --json — detects affected projects that have a lint target. 

npm run affected:lint:ci — runs the configured lint target for affected projects. 

4. Lint Configuration 

Linting must be configured for the applications and libraries protected by the harness. 

ESLint and/or Stylelint must be configured in the relevant libraries and applications. 

A lint target must be defined in the corresponding project.json files. 

3. High-Level Architecture 

The harness follows a staged pipeline architecture. Each stage has a clearly defined responsibility, allowing individual components to evolve independently without affecting the overall workflow. 

Pipeline: 

Trigger → Orchestration → Decision/Guards → Validation → Failure Intelligence → Remediation Handoff → Commit Retry 

Developer Commit 
      │ 
      ▼ 
┌──────────────────┐ 
│  Trigger Layer   │ 
└────────┬─────────┘ 
         ▼ 
┌──────────────────────┐ 
│ Orchestration Layer  │ 
└────────┬─────────────┘ 
         ▼ 
┌─────────────────────────┐ 
│ Decision Layer (Guards) │ 
└────────┬────────────────┘ 
    ┌────┴────┐ 
  Skip       Run 
    │         │ 
    │         ▼ 
    │   ┌──────────────────┐ 
    │   │ Validation Layer │ 
    │   └────────┬─────────┘ 
    │       ┌────┴────┐ 
    │     Pass       Fail 
    │       │         │ 
    │       │         ▼ 
    │       │   ┌──────────────────────┐ 
    │       │   │ Failure Intelligence │ 
    │       │   └──────────┬───────────┘ 
    │       │              ▼ 
    │       │   ┌──────────────────────┐ 
    │       │   │ Remediation Handoff  │ 
