    │       │   │ Commit Retry Layer   │ 
    │       │   └──────────┬───────────┘ 
    └───────┴──────────────┘ 
                   ▼ 
             Commit Allowed 

4. Core Components 

4.1 Trigger Layer 

The Trigger Layer receives the commit event and starts the harness lifecycle. 

Responsibilities: 

Start pre-commit execution. 

Pass control to the Orchestration Layer. 

Enforce synchronous completion before the commit is finalized. 

Return the final harness status to Git. 

4.2 Orchestration Layer 

The Orchestration Layer coordinates execution across all stages of the harness. 

Responsibilities: 

Define and enforce the execution order. 

Capture command output and exit status. 

Normalize success and failure behavior. 

Handle transitions between stages. 

Guarantee deterministic exit states. 

4.3 Decision Layer — Guards 

The Decision Layer determines whether validation is required for the current commit. Its purpose is to reduce unnecessary validation cost while maintaining confidence in the quality gate. 

Responsibilities: 

Inspect staged changes. 

Identify modified file types and project areas. 

Determine whether quality-relevant scopes are impacted. 

Skip validation when the changes cannot affect guarded quality dimensions. 

Route relevant changes to the Validation Layer. 

4.4 Validation Layer 

The Validation Layer executes the quality checks defined by project policy. 

Responsibilities: 

Execute lint and other configured quality commands. 

Stream relevant feedback to the developer. 

Capture command output. 

Return machine-consumable pass/fail results. 

Preserve original validation evidence for downstream processing. 

4.5 Failure Intelligence Layer 

Raw lint output can contain a significant amount of noise. The Failure Intelligence Layer converts this output into concise, actionable failure context. 

Responsibilities: 

Extract error-focused information from command output. 

Separate actionable errors from informational or irrelevant logs. 

Persist the latest failure artifact. 

Maintain historical failure artifacts where required. 

Build structured remediation context. 

Provide a consistent representation of the failure to developers and automation. 

4.6 Remediation Handoff Layer 

The Remediation Handoff Layer initiates the guided fix workflow after validation failure. 

Responsibilities: 

Open a remediation context using the captured failure evidence. 

Provide relevant errors and surrounding context. 

Initiate automated or assisted remediation where applicable. 

Present an explicit decision point to the developer. 

Prevent silent progression without human approval. 

4.7 Commit Retry Layer 

Once remediation has been accepted, the Commit Retry Layer resumes the commit workflow. 

Responsibilities: 

Stage corrected changes. 

Re-attempt the commit. 

Re-run the harness through the normal commit lifecycle. 

Ensure the final repository state passes the same quality gate. 

Design Principle: 

Prefer safe skipping only when there is high confidence that the guarded quality outcomes cannot be affected. 

For example, a commit containing only documentation changes may not require execution of a TypeScript or Javascript linting process. 

Validation behavior: 

Pass 
  ↓ 
Validation Successful 
  ↓ 
Commit continues 
 
Fail 
  ↓ 
Validation Failed 
  ↓ 
Commit is blocked 
  ↓ 
Failure processing begins 

Failure context may include: 

Failure Summary 
├── Validation Type 
├── Failed Rule 
├── File 
├── Line / Column 
├── Error Message 
├── Severity 
└── Suggested Remediation Context 

Remediation workflow: 

Failure 
   ↓ 
Analyze 
   ↓ 
Propose / Apply Fix 
   ↓ 
Human Review 
   ├── Accept 
   └── Rework 

Commit retry workflow: 

Fix Accepted 
     ↓ 
Stage Changes 
     ↓ 
Retry Commit 
     ↓ 
Run Guards 
     ↓ 
Run Validation 
     ↓ 
Pass → Commit 
Fail → Remediation 

5. Key Architectural Concepts 

5.1 Deterministic Gating 

The harness follows a clear pass/fail model. There are no ambiguous intermediate states where the harness silently allows a commit despite an unresolved quality failure. 

Meets Policy → Continue Commit 
Fails Policy → Block Commit 

5.2 Progressive Cost Control 

Validation is performed progressively based on risk. Low-cost guard checks execute first, while expensive validation is performed only when the changed content can affect the guarded quality dimensions. 

Cheap Checks → Guard Evaluation → Expensive Validation → Failure Analysis / Remediation 

5.3 Context Compression 

The harness converts potentially large and noisy validation logs into high-signal failure artifacts. This reduces diagnosis time and provides automation with predictable input. 

Raw Output → Parse → Filter → Structure → Actionable Failure Context 

5.4 Human-Governed Automation 

Automation can analyze failures and assist with remediation, but it does not have unrestricted authority to progress the workflow. Remediation requires explicit human approval. 

Harness detects issue → Automation analyzes → Fix proposed/applied → Human reviews → Human approves → Retry 

5.5 Replayable Recovery 

Remediation does not bypass the quality gate. After a fix is accepted, the harness re-enters the normal commit path and validates the final content again. 

Fix Accepted → Stage Changes → Retry Commit → Run Guards → Run Validation → Pass / Remediation 

6. Design Principles 

Fail closed — unresolved quality failures block the commit. 

Validate only when necessary — use reliable guards to avoid redundant work. 

Make failures actionable — provide structured context instead of raw logs. 

Keep stages loosely coupled — each layer should have a focused responsibility. 

Keep humans in control — remediation requires explicit approval. 

Never bypass validation after remediation — accepted fixes must pass the normal gate. 

Prefer deterministic behavior — identical inputs should produce predictable outcomes. 

Optimize the feedback loop — minimize unnecessary execution and investigation time. 

7. How to set up 

7.1 Setup harness code 

Download the ZIP file (Lint Harness) and extract the content inside the root folder of your nx-based project.  

7.2 Setup hooks 

Open terminal and execute following command: 
    git config core.hooksPath .githooks  

To verify if the hooks are ready to be used, execute following command: 

    git config --get core.hooksPath 

Terminal out should display: 
    .githooks 

7.1 Run the harness 

Generate a change in your codebase. 

Stage the changes and start a commit to remote branch. 

Observe the harness being executed as part of pre-commit hook. 

If failures are detected, a Copilot chat session will start and will resolve all the detected issues. 

8. Overall Outcome 

The Pre-Commit Lint Harness establishes a controlled quality pipeline between developer changes and repository history. 

Intelligent Change Detection + Deterministic Validation + Structured Failure Analysis + Guided Remediation + Human Approval + End-to-End Retry 

This approach allows the harness to enforce coding standards consistently while minimizing developer friction and creating a foundation that can be extended beyond linting to additional quality and policy checks. 
